{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'cited_views|def .*judg|dependencies' src/hivemind -g '*.py' | rg 'loop|judge|matching' | head -55; sed -n '296,331p;553,600p;723,757p;1117,1154p' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/library-pack.md\"","aggregated_output":"src/hivemind/service.py:528:def verdict_for(comparator, judged, yi):\nsrc/hivemind/bundle.py:65:def not_judged(views_db, view_id, created_seq, as_of=None):\nsrc/hivemind/bundle.py:172:            'judgements': judgements, 'evidence': evidence, 'dependencies': dependencies, 'dependants': dependants,\nsrc/hivemind/idea.py:120:def judged(ledger, view_id, as_of, detailed=False):\nsrc/hivemind/vocabulary.py:67:def judgement_answer(data):\nsrc/hivemind/views/judging.py:284:def round_of_judgements(archive, cfg, conn, changed_views, seq, item, *, transport=None):\nsrc/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:212:def ask_collision_judge(archive, cfg, conn, state, peers, llm_transport):\nsrc/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:269:            data['candidates'].append({'id': c['id'], 'outcome': fixed[0], 'target_view_id': fixed[1], 'dependencies': [],\nsrc/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:351:        data['candidates'].append({'id': c['id'], 'outcome': outcome, 'target_view_id': target or '', 'dependencies': [],\nsrc/hivemind/views/round.py:73:def judge_day(conn, archive, cfg, day, seq, *, transport=None, log=print):\nsrc/hivemind/config.py:20:def _prompt_files(extraction, matching, rejudging, idea=None, idea_summary=None, collision=None, placement=None):\nsrc/hivemind/views/store.py:18:    'judgements': 'judgement_id integer primary key autoincrement, view_id text, seq integer, input_id text, rung integer, reasons text, cited_evidence_json text, cited_views_json text, pushes_json text, changed integer, why_changed text, config_checksum text, call_id integer, request_sha256 text, publication_day text, theme_pushes_json text, reference text, implied_outcome text',\nsrc/hivemind/views/store.py:212:def has_judgement(conn, view_id, seq):\nsrc/hivemind/views/store.py:395:def recent_judgements(conn, view_id, n):\nsrc/hivemind/views/matching.py:4:succession unique and oriented, dependencies among shown views or a waited year, no cycles).\nsrc/hivemind/views/matching.py:39:                'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False, 'required': ['id', 'outcome', 'target_view_id', 'reason', 'dependencies'],\nsrc/hivemind/views/matching.py:43:                    'dependencies': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False,\nsrc/hivemind/views/matching.py:236:    parts.append('Return, per the JSON schema, each candidate\\'s destination and dependencies, and one decision for every evidence → thesis pair listed above (id and view_id exactly as listed). Empty arrays when there is nothing. An absent target is an empty string.')\nsrc/hivemind/views/matching.py:261:    outcomes: dict         # candidate id -> (outcome, target or None, dependencies list)\nsrc/hivemind/views/matching.py:341:            raw_deps[c['id']] = d.get('dependencies', [])\nsrc/hivemind/views/matching.py:347:    # dependencies, once every candidate's outcome is known\nsrc/hivemind/views/matching.py:349:    for cid, dependencies in raw_deps.items():\nsrc/hivemind/views/matching.py:352:        for dep in dependencies:\nsrc/hivemind/views/matching.py:384:                refusals.append(f'candidate {cid}: its dependencies lead back to itself')\n## v8: How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?\n\n- Looks at: NVIDIA stand-alone CPU sales\n- Opened 2026-07-05 from: AMD Stock and Intel Crushed Nvidia in the First Half. Here's My Prediction for the Second Half. (fool.com); due 2027-02-28; open\n- Research groups: none\n- Answer ladder:\n  1. Strongly bullish: Stand-alone CPU sales exceed $25 billion in FY2027.\n  2. Bullish: Stand-alone CPU sales are at least $20 billion and no more than $25 billion in FY2027.\n  3. Neutral: Stand-alone CPU sales are at least $15 billion and less than $20 billion in FY2027.  <== current\n  4. Bearish: Stand-alone CPU sales are at least $10 billion and less than $15 billion in FY2027.\n  5. Strongly bearish: Stand-alone CPU sales are less than $10 billion in FY2027.\n- History: 66 judgements; first judged 2026-07-05 at rung 2; rung moves: 2026-07-12 2->3, 2026-07-21 3->5, 2026-07-22 5->3, 2026-07-28 3->2, 2026-08-01 2->3, 2026-08-03 3->2, 2026-09-16 2->4, 2026-09-17 4->3\n- Latest judgement 2026-09-20, why changed: The rung and implied estimate remain around $18 billion, but AWS's receipt of its first Vera CPU server is a new execution milestone that strengthens the evidence of deployment; it does not provide the period-specific volume needed to lift the forecast to $20 billion.\n- Latest reasoning: The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU sales guidance, which sits at rung 2; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. The evidence supports a substantial ramp but still implies about $18 billion: Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each is roughly $6.5 billion, while AWS's CPU quantity is only described as millions without a purchase period and OCI's hundreds-of-thousands plan is not a FY2027 revenue figure. These disclosures, alongside shipments underway, support growth beyond the tracked base but do not establish the roughly 4 million units needed for $20 billion at that ASP. Thus the implied figure remains below the rung-2 boundary and within rung 3. The FY2028 doubling outlook is not a FY2027 estimate, and AWS receiving its first server improves evidence of execution without quantifying the FY2027 volume enough to move the forecast across a boundary.\n- Evidence in force: 77 (party_statement 51, third_party_view 14, fact 11, rumour 1)\n- Evidence cited by the latest judgement:\n  - e897 [party_statement; 2026-07-24; push toward_first] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the nearly $20 billion CPU revenue visibility for FY2027 is for standalone CPU sales. | source: NVIDIA Says Its CPU Revenue Sits Outside the $1 Trillion Forecast. Here’s Where the Stock Could Go (tikr.com) input gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b\n  - e1414 [party_statement; 2026-08-03; push toward_first] NVIDIA guides to around $20 billion in Vera sales in FY2027. | source: Nvidia (NVDA) Uses Own Chips for Design, While AMD (AMD) Relies on Rivals (finance.yahoo.com) input gnews:8c813e909e7c0c5e7535e616\n  - e666 [third_party_view; 2026-07-21; push toward_last] Wolfe Research forecast a Vera CPU average selling price of about $5,000 per chip and shipments of about 1.3 million units in calendar 2026. | source: Nvidia details its next-generation Vera CPU for AI, setting up challenge to AMD and Intel (cnbc.com) input gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c\n  - e668 [party_statement; 2026-07-21; push toward_first] NVIDIA said OpenAI planned to deploy Vera CPUs in large quantities starting in calendar Q3 2026. | source: Nvidia details its next-generation Vera CPU for AI, setting up challenge to AMD and Intel (cnbc.com) input gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c\n  - e2543 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] NVIDIA said Vera CPU shipments were underway to lead partners including OCI and SpaceX AI, with AWS shipments starting in Q2 FY2027, and expected deployment by every major hyperscaler, NeoCloud, AI lab, and system OEM. | source: NVDA Q2 FY2027 Earnings Call (NVIDIA Corporation) input truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26\n  - e2544 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] NVIDIA estimated total server CPU demand at approximately $20 billion and preliminarily expected its CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028. | source: NVDA Q2 FY2027 Earnings Call (NVIDIA Corporation) input truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26\n  - e2636 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] Jensen Huang said Amazon Web Services was going to buy millions of CPUs; no purchase period or exact quantity was specified. | source: CNBC | VERSANT Press Room (cnbc.com) input extra:b23f73a08a535993119430f5\n  - e2903 [fact; 2026-08-27; push toward_first] AWS received its first NVIDIA Vera CPU server and Vera Rubin GPU. | source: Delivering Vera: NVIDIA’s First CPU Built for Agents Is Shipping Now | NVIDIA Blog (NVIDIA Corporation) input nvblog:vera-cpu-delivery\n  - e2904 [party_statement; 2026-08-27; push toward_first] Oracle Cloud Infrastructure plans to deploy hundreds of thousands of NVIDIA Vera CPUs beginning in 2026. | source: Delivering Vera: NVIDIA’s First CPU Built for Agents Is Shipping Now | NVIDIA Blog (NVIDIA Corporation) input nvblog:vera-cpu-delivery\n  - e3087 [fact; 2026-08-28; push toward_first] AWS agreed to buy 2 million NVIDIA GPUs and adopt NVIDIA's Vera CPU, with some GPUs integrated with Rubin. | source: NVIDIA (NVDA) Tops Q2 Estimates and Guides Above Forecasts, But Shares Slip (insidermonkey.com) input gnews:d341dec96cd1045ab0e0a92f\n  - e4398 [party_statement; 2026-09-10; push toward_first] NVIDIA was already shipping Vera CPUs to key customers and expected $20 billion in Vera CPU sales in FY2027. | source: An Nvidia Move Just Put AMD and Intel on the Defensive (fool.com) input gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a\n  - e4553 [party_statement; 2026-09-12; push toward_first] NVIDIA management reiterated potential Vera CPU revenue of $20 billion in FY2027 and said it could more than double in FY2028. | source: 40,000 Employees Today and 4 Million Agents Tomorrow: Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang Believes Agentic AI Will Soon Become the Norm in the Workplace (fool.com) input gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10\n  - e4797 [party_statement; 2026-09-16; push toward_last] NVIDIA estimates the total annual server CPU market at approximately $20 billion. | source: Why It’s Time to Load Up on Nvidia Stock (barchart.com) input gnews:5c5a96c670714dee86769d87\n- A random sample of uncited evidence in force (seed 20261120):\n  - e939 [third_party_view; 2026-07-25] The author estimates Intel and AMD currently hold 67% and 33%, respectively, of the server CPU market. | source: Micron, Nvidia, or SpaceX: Which Is the Best AI Stock to Put $1,000 in Right Now? (fool.com) input gnews:2dbdeb0f4121e37164fd6ccd\n  - e2715 [party_statement; 2026-08-26] NVIDIA had said it expected CPU sales to reach $20 billion in the current fiscal year, FY2027. | source: Jensen Huang Just Delivered Great News to Nvidia Shareholders (fool.com) input gnews:58da18a5e0a271a50e9bbdaf\n  - e938 [fact; 2026-07-25] SpaceX, OpenAI, and Anthropic are evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU; NVIDIA says it delivers 50% better performance for AI agents than the Intel- and AMD-used x86 architecture. | source: Micron, Nvidia, or SpaceX: Which Is the Best AI Stock to Put $1,000 in Right Now? (fool.com) input gnews:2dbdeb0f4121e37164fd6ccd\n  - e3416 [party_statement; 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA projected $20 billion in CPU sales for FY2027. | source: Nvidia Just Delivered Bad News for AMD and Intel (fool.com) input gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55\n  - e4998 [party_statement; 2026-09-18] NVIDIA anticipates a large increase in server processor revenue. | source: Global AI Infrastructure Spending Will Hit $31.6 Trillion by 2050. This Is the Stock That Could Benefit the Most (fool.com) input gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca\n\n## v16: How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?\n\n- Looks at: NVIDIA total CPU revenue in FY2027\n- Opened 2026-07-15 from: Is NVIDIA’s Next Big Bet A Massive Pivot? (trefis.com); due 2027-02-28; open\n- Research groups: none\n- Answer ladder:\n  1. Strongly bullish: Total CPU revenue is greater than $25 billion.  <== current\n  2. Bullish: Total CPU revenue is greater than $20 billion and at most $25 billion.\n  3. Neutral: Total CPU revenue is at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion.\n  4. Bearish: Total CPU revenue is at least $10 billion and less than $15 billion.\n  5. Strongly bearish: Total CPU revenue is less than $10 billion.\n- History: 56 judgements; first judged 2026-07-15 at rung 3; rung moves: 2026-08-28 3->1\n- Latest judgement 2026-09-20, why changed: The rung remains 1, but later evidence adds support for execution of the FY2027 Vera outlook—shipments to named customers, full production, and an AWS adoption agreement—and supplies further competitive-performance evidence. These change the supporting facts, though they do not establish a quantified change to the FY2027 revenue estimate.\n- Latest reasoning: The reference is NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3. The most likely total is just above $25 billion: the specific FY2027 Vera outlook is about $20 billion, and Grace had already exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis during FY2027; carrying that recent Grace run-rate through the fiscal year puts the total slightly above the rung-1 boundary. Vera shipments and full production support the outlook, though the estimate is close to the boundary: Wolfe’s 2026 shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, and AMD’s performance claims are competitive risks without a quantified revision to NVIDIA’s FY2027 outlook. The approximately $20 billion server-CPU demand figure is a headwind, but it is not an explicit cap on NVIDIA’s specific Vera sales outlook.\n- Evidence in force: 86 (party_statement 50, third_party_view 25, fact 11)\n- Evidence cited by the latest judgement:\n  - e666 [third_party_view; 2026-07-21; push toward_last] Wolfe Research forecast a Vera CPU average selling price of about $5,000 per chip and shipments of about 1.3 million units in calendar 2026. | source: Nvidia details its next-generation Vera CPU for AI, setting up challenge to AMD and Intel (cnbc.com) input gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c\n  - e668 [party_statement; 2026-07-21; push toward_first] NVIDIA said OpenAI planned to deploy Vera CPUs in large quantities starting in calendar Q3 2026. | source: Nvidia details its next-generation Vera CPU for AI, setting up challenge to AMD and Intel (cnbc.com) input gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c\n  - e723 [fact; 2026-07-21; push toward_first] NVIDIA said it delivered Vera chips to OpenAI, Anthropic, and SpaceX in June 2026. | source: Nvidia is releasing full specs for its AI server CPU, taking aim at AMD and Intel (qz.com) input gnews:9ec05dc5e996b554b6f7a5f2\n  - e724 [fact; 2026-07-21; push toward_first] Hewlett Packard Enterprise unveiled the Vera-powered ProLiant Compute DL394 Gen12 server, with availability expected in fall 2026. | source: Nvidia is releasing full specs for its AI server CPU, taking aim at AMD and Intel (qz.com) input gnews:9ec05dc5e996b554b6f7a5f2\n  - e794 [third_party_view; 2026-07-22; push toward_last] AMD estimates EPYC 9965 delivers approximately 2.4 times the rack-level throughput of NVIDIA Vera in a modeled 100kW deployment, and projects EPYC 6 at roughly 3.3 times Vera's total throughput. | source: BofA sees server CPU TAM hitting $170bn by 2030 as NVIDIA takes on AMD (au.finance.yahoo.com) input gnews:55b3b854d30ecb73679c2088\n  - e896 [party_statement; 2026-07-24; push none] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera opens a $200 billion market for NVIDIA and that the company has visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, FY2027. | source: NVIDIA Says Its CPU Revenue Sits Outside the $1 Trillion Forecast. Here’s Where the Stock Could Go (tikr.com) input gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b\n  - e897 [party_statement; 2026-07-24; push toward_first] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the nearly $20 billion CPU revenue visibility for FY2027 is for standalone CPU sales. | source: NVIDIA Says Its CPU Revenue Sits Outside the $1 Trillion Forecast. Here’s Where the Stock Could Go (tikr.com) input gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b\n  - e2311 [party_statement; 2026-08-24; push toward_first] NVIDIA expects stand-alone Vera server CPU revenue of $20 billion in FY2027. | source: Where Will Nvidia Stock Be in 5 Years? (fool.com) input gnews:6c6c4a4aa3b22bf433133e4d\n  - e2542 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] NVIDIA Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis as of the Q2 FY2027 call. | source: NVDA Q2 FY2027 Earnings Call (NVIDIA Corporation) input truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26\n  - e2543 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] NVIDIA said Vera CPU shipments were underway to lead partners including OCI and SpaceX AI, with AWS shipments starting in Q2 FY2027, and expected deployment by every major hyperscaler, NeoCloud, AI lab, and system OEM. | source: NVDA Q2 FY2027 Earnings Call (NVIDIA Corporation) input truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26\n  - e2544 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] NVIDIA estimated total server CPU demand at approximately $20 billion and preliminarily expected its CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028. | source: NVDA Q2 FY2027 Earnings Call (NVIDIA Corporation) input truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26\n  - e2636 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] Jensen Huang said Amazon Web Services was going to buy millions of CPUs; no purchase period or exact quantity was specified. | source: CNBC | VERSANT Press Room (cnbc.com) input extra:b23f73a08a535993119430f5\n  - e2735 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] NVIDIA said Vera CPU was in full production and expected total CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028, citing customer demand and an improving supply outlook. | source: NVIDIA Q2 Earnings Call Highlights (marketbeat.com) input gnews:7dfab0010a8ac15786f05387\n  - e3007 [fact; 2026-08-27; push toward_first] Amazon Web Services may buy millions of NVIDIA Vera CPUs; the purchase is conditional and no date was specified. | source: Jim Cramer says Nvidia and Salesforce earnings upended two bear narratives (cnbc.com) input gnews:fa03217ed7ae3070a82acd79\n  - e3087 [fact; 2026-08-28; push toward_first] AWS agreed to buy 2 million NVIDIA GPUs and adopt NV