{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sqlite3 -readonly poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/archive.sqlite '.tables'; rg -n 'D208 ·|dependency.*supported|D53[7-9]|D54[0-2]' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | cut -c1-250 | tail -20\"","aggregated_output":"model_calls\n2638:## D208 · 2026-10-28 · The thesis tree: root quantities, a seeded driver layer per company, shared macro themes with declared channels (design, not built)\n6457:## D537 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the dependency-selection census has one clear case and negative controls; before any send, the edge's effect on filing and judging is proved on stored data (council consensus, reflection round 28)\n6479:## D538 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: one dependency edge shows the intended conclusion to the judge and removes seven filing links; the dependency slice's design is opened at $0, beginning with the separation of the edge's two uses (council consen\n6491:- **The council's review:** the filing stop condition of D537 is met. A lost filing is not a demonstrated changed answer. The\n6504:## D539 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the separation of a dependency edge's two uses is kept as a candidate, not yet the builder's contract; its design continues at $0 (council consensus, reflection round 30)\n6525:## D540 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the separation stays a provisional research candidate; the filing and duplicate-label contracts are finished at $0 before any selection design or send (council consensus, reflection round 31)\n6544:## D541 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the seed edge's label stops everything that touches a seed edge; the filing finding is bounded; the unseeded failure-and-reader contract is finished at $0 before the separation is called sound (council consensu\n6566:## D542 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the dependency stretch is closed with the separation recorded as a provisional candidate; the status is consolidated; nothing is sent (council consensus, reflection round 33)\n6568:- **Done at $0 after D541** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/SEPARATION-DESIGN.md` section 2.6): the\n6579:- **Decided, citing D439:** the dependency stretch (D533 to D542) is closed. The separation is a provisional candidate, not\n10348:  - D368 item 2 (dependencies chosen at matching) stays open defect 4 in `CURRENT_STATE.md`, closed as a stretch by D542. None of the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"PYTHONPATH=src python3 -B -c 'from pathlib import Path\nimport sqlite3, json, re\nfrom hivemind import bundle, service\nbase=Path('\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17'\\\\'')\nchoices={1:('\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v8'\\\\''),2:('\\\\''v8'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v16'\\\\'','\\\\''v17'\\\\'','\\\\''v21'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\''),3:('\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\'')}\nfor run, vids in choices.items():\n with service.open_ledger(base/f'\\\\''run/old-{run}/prod/views.sqlite'\\\\'') as db:\n  data=bundle.library(db,None,'\\\\''2026-09-20'\\\\'')\n  vs={v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']:v for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\'']}\n  print('\\\\''old'\\\\'',run,'\\\\''views'\\\\'',len(vs),'\\\\''deps'\\\\'',sum(bool(v['\\\\''dependencies'\\\\'']) for v in vs.values()),'\\\\''judgements'\\\\'',sum(len(v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\'']) for v in vs.values()),'\\\\''citing others'\\\\'',sum(bool(j['\\\\''cited_views'\\\\'']) for v in vs.values() for j in v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\'']),flush=True)\n  for vid in vids:\n   v=vs[vid]; j=v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''][-1]\n   print(vid, j['\\\\''day'\\\\''],j['\\\\''id'\\\\''],j['\\\\''seq'\\\\''],v['\\\\''question'\\\\''],j['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''],flush=True)\n   if run==3: print('\\\\''current band'\\\\'',next(r for r in v['\\\\''rungs'\\\\''] if r['\\\\''position'\\\\'']==j['\\\\''rung'\\\\'']))\n  if run==2:\n   v=vs['\\\\''v9'\\\\'']\n   for j in v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\'']:\n    if j['\\\\''day'\\\\''] in ('\\\\''2026-09-12'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-09-13'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-09-14'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-09-15'\\\\''): print('\\\\''shift'\\\\'',j['\\\\''day'\\\\''],j['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''][:510])\n   print('\\\\''414 items'\\\\'',[(e['\\\\''id'\\\\''],e['\\\\''one_line'\\\\''][:125]) for e in v['\\\\''evidence'\\\\''] if re.search(r'\\\\''(?<\"'!'\"\\\\d)414(?\"'!'\"\\\\d)'\\\\'',e['\\\\''one_line'\\\\'']+'\\\\'' '\\\\''+e['\\\\''quote'\\\\''])])\n   print('\\\\''counts'\\\\'',[(vid,sum(a['\\\\''rung'\\\\'']\"'!=b['\"'\\\\''rung'\\\\''] for a,b in zip(vs[vid]['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''],vs[vid]['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''][1:]))) for vid in ('\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v8'\\\\'','\\\\''v5'\\\\'','\\\\''v10'\\\\'')])\n   for v in sorted(vs.values(), key=lambda v: ((v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''][-1]['\\\\''day'\\\\''] if v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''] else v['\\\\''created_day'\\\\'']),v['\\\\''created_seq'\\\\'']), reverse=True)[:5]:\n    print('\\\\''rank'\\\\'',v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''],v['\\\\''question'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sqlite3 -readonly poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/archive.sqlite '.schema model_calls'; sed -n '2643,2658p;6579,6589p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; sed -n '44,62p;91,108p' poc/ui/src/components/library.js\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sqlite3 -readonly poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/archive.sqlite '.schema model_calls'; sed -n '2643,2658p;6579,6589p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; sed -n '44,62p;91,108p' poc/ui/src/components/library.js\"","aggregated_output":"CREATE TABLE model_calls (\n  call_id integer primary key autoincrement, purpose text, config_version text, config_checksum text, model text,\n  request_sha256 text unique, request_json text, response_json text, answer_json text, prompt_chars integer,\n  input_tokens integer, output_tokens integer, cost_usd real, seconds real, created_at text\n);\nCREATE INDEX model_calls_sha on model_calls(request_sha256);\n\n## D209 · 2026-10-28 · A statement no open question takes is dropped at extraction; evaluation reads the dropped record, not a side list\n\n- Alex, on the `unplaced_statements` table D207 had added: \"If you think about yourself as a hedge fund analyst, if it doesn't matter, just\n  fucking throw it away. That's the original design. Why are you keeping it on the side? It breaks the entire design. … But if there are\n  false positives that got thrown away in this side list, you got to pick it up. Again, that's the way to do proper evaluation. So don't\n  do something that is like halfway done and just to cherry pick and get the result in numbers.\"\n- Decided: the placer's unfiled statements go to the handover's `dropped` list with the reason `no home`, like every other drop; the\n  `unplaced` field, the `unplaced_statements` table and `store.add_unplaced` are removed; no re-placement mechanism is built. The\n  dropped record (every drop with its reason, already in every handover) is where evaluation reads what was thrown away.\n- Evaluation done the same evening on the acceptance week's 630 drops, read one by one\n  ([thrown-away-read.md](research/2026-10-28-extraction-quality/results/thrown-away-read.md)): 393 junk (62%), 236 material; of the\n  material, 87% had no standing question to go to (the AI Compute Partnership question alone 96: identical request bytes proposed it in\n  1, 2 and 0 candidates across three runs), 2% were statements of the article that opened the question (the placer never sees the\n  item's candidates), 4% the placer left out with the home shown (two self-admitted mistakes, three where the stopgap rack NVL72x2 was\n  not recognised as the Kyber/Rubin Ultra matter), 7% marginal.\n- **Decided, citing D439:** the dependency stretch (D533 to D542) is closed. The separation is a provisional candidate, not\n  sound for build; if the slice is reopened its review must settle the cycle through an awaited question at creation, closing\n  behaviour, a theme shown twice, the historical readers and the label's meaning before any build. No selection call is\n  designed and nothing is sent. Claude's proposed general rule (\"a contract that depends on an unbuilt piece is written only\n  when that piece opens\") is **not** adopted: some such contracts decide whether the pieces can coexist, as the filing and\n  reading conflict did; AGENTS §2 still requires the contracts complete before code. The next task is the consolidation of the\n  status and the decision record (one next-task statement; production the default and both systems kept; D375 and D368 decided\n  and awaiting implementation and acceptance). No open investigation outranks that closure on the choice between the systems;\n  a new step worth a decision returns to the council under D439.\n\n## D543 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the cold reflection on the lean path: D518 stands with its reasons, the choice of system is not made in Alex's place, and no investigation is opened (reflection council consensus)\n        onChange(value) {\n          state.object = value;\n          state.limit = 24;\n          draw();\n        },\n      }),\n      createSelect(find('[data-status-filter]'), {\n        label: 'Status',\n        value: state.status,\n        options: [\n          { value: '', label: 'All statuses' },\n          { value: 'Open', label: 'Open' },\n          { value: 'Closed', label: 'Closed' },\n        ],\n        onChange(value) {\n          state.status = value;\n          state.limit = 24;\n          draw();\n        },\n      ) +\n      `<div class=\"sidebar-kicker sidebar-kicker--groups\">Research groups</div>` +\n      groups.map((g) => nav(g.id, g.name, g.count)).join('');\n    const rows = librarySelection(data, state);\n    const cards = find('[data-cards]');\n    cards.replaceChildren();\n    const start =\n      state.range === '30'\n        ? [data.days[0] || state.day, addDays(state.day, -29)].sort().at(-1)\n        : data.days[0] || state.day;\n    for (const thesis of rows.slice(0, state.limit)) {\n      const card = createThesisCard(snapshotThesis(thesis, state.day), {\n        day: state.day,\n        start,\n        end: state.day,\n        scope,\n        onOpen: (id) => details.open(id, state.day),\n      });\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"PYTHONPATH=src python3 -B -c 'from pathlib import Path\nimport sqlite3, json, re\nfrom hivemind import bundle, service\nbase=Path('\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17'\\\\'')\nchoices={1:('\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v8'\\\\''),2:('\\\\''v8'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v16'\\\\'','\\\\''v17'\\\\'','\\\\''v21'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\''),3:('\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\'')}\nfor run, vids in choices.items():\n with service.open_ledger(base/f'\\\\''run/old-{run}/prod/views.sqlite'\\\\'') as db:\n  data=bundle.library(db,None,'\\\\''2026-09-20'\\\\'')\n  vs={v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']:v for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\'']}\n  print('\\\\''old'\\\\'',run,'\\\\''views'\\\\'',len(vs),'\\\\''deps'\\\\'',sum(bool(v['\\\\''dependencies'\\\\'']) for v in vs.values()),'\\\\''judgements'\\\\'',sum(len(v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\'']) for v in vs.values()),'\\\\''citing others'\\\\'',sum(bool(j['\\\\''cited_views'\\\\'']) for v in vs.values() for j in v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\'']),flush=True)\n  for vid in vids:\n   v=vs[vid]; j=v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''][-1]\n   print(vid, j['\\\\''day'\\\\''],j['\\\\''id'\\\\''],j['\\\\''seq'\\\\''],v['\\\\''question'\\\\''],j['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''],flush=True)\n   if run==3: print('\\\\''current band'\\\\'',next(r for r in v['\\\\''rungs'\\\\''] if r['\\\\''position'\\\\'']==j['\\\\''rung'\\\\'']))\n  if run==2:\n   v=vs['\\\\''v9'\\\\'']\n   for j in v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\'']:\n    if j['\\\\''day'\\\\''] in ('\\\\''2026-09-12'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-09-13'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-09-14'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-09-15'\\\\''): print('\\\\''shift'\\\\'',j['\\\\''day'\\\\''],j['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''][:510])\n   print('\\\\''414 items'\\\\'',[(e['\\\\''id'\\\\''],e['\\\\''one_line'\\\\''][:125]) for e in v['\\\\''evidence'\\\\''] if re.search(r'\\\\''(?<\"'!'\"\\\\d)414(?\"'!'\"\\\\d)'\\\\'',e['\\\\''one_line'\\\\'']+'\\\\'' '\\\\''+e['\\\\''quote'\\\\''])])\n   print('\\\\''counts'\\\\'',[(vid,sum(a['\\\\''rung'\\\\'']\"'!=b['\"'\\\\''rung'\\\\''] for a,b in zip(vs[vid]['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''],vs[vid]['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''][1:]))) for vid in ('\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v8'\\\\'','\\\\''v5'\\\\'','\\\\''v10'\\\\'')])\n   for v in sorted(vs.values(), key=lambda v: ((v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''][-1]['\\\\''day'\\\\''] if v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''] else v['\\\\''created_day'\\\\'']),v['\\\\''created_seq'\\\\'']), reverse=True)[:5]:\n    print('\\\\''rank'\\\\'',v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''],v['\\\\''question'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"old 1 views 47 deps 0 judgements 1609 citing others 0\nv4 2026-09-20 1575 1884 What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027? The market reference is Wall Street consensus of about $411 billion, which is rung 2. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion (rung 3), but the current-quarter guidance and the specific Q4 growth estimate imply a higher outcome: about $404 billion. That is above rung 3's $392 billion boundary but below rung 1's $431.2 billion threshold. Supply constraints and competition temper the upside; the evidence does not imply enough additional revenue to cross rung 1.\nv8 2026-09-20 1580 1884 How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027? The reference is Morningstar’s $361 billion FY2027 Data Center estimate, which is in rung 2. The implied outcome is higher, at about $390 billion, but remains below rung 1’s $400 billion boundary: the reported first half is $164 billion, Q3 is about $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue midpoint and the recent 92.5% Data Center mix, and I estimate Q4 at about $126 billion, allowing for the stated Q4 reacceleration and Vera Rubin supply build while discounting for supply and infrastructure constraints. Thus the evidence supports a substantial premium to the reference but does not imply crossing $400 billion.\nold 2 views 43 deps 0 judgements 1616 citing others 0\nv8 2026-09-20 1616 1884 How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027? The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU sales guidance, which sits at rung 2; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. The evidence supports a substantial ramp but still implies about $18 billion: Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each is roughly $6.5 billion, while AWS's CPU quantity is only described as millions without a purchase period and OCI's hundreds-of-thousands plan is not a FY2027 revenue figure. These disclosures, alongside shipments underway, support growth beyond the tracked base but do not establish the roughly 4 million units needed for $20 billion at that ASP. Thus the implied figure remains below the rung-2 boundary and within rung 3. The FY2028 doubling outlook is not a FY2027 estimate, and AWS receiving its first server improves evidence of execution without quantifying the FY2027 volume enough to move the forecast across a boundary.\nv9 2026-09-20 1592 1884 How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027? The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is now around $410 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion in the first half, Q3 total-revenue guidance and the recent Data Center mix imply roughly $100 billion for Q3, and a roughly $146 billion Q4 is supported by the expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin ramp. SemiAnalysis's forecast of second-half Data Center revenue 20% above analyst estimates is a meaningful upside input: applying it to the roughly $250 billion of second-half revenue implied by the $414 billion reference would point to about $464 billion for the year. I discount that forecast because it does not state its underlying estimate and its implied Q4 is much higher than the company has quantified; the resulting central estimate remains near $410 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. Supply and competition risks, and NVIDIA's exclusion of China compute revenue from its outlook, temper the upside.\nv14 2026-09-20 1585 1884 How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027? The reference is about $411 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2; the cited Zacks growth estimate also implies about $405 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate implies about $419 billion: estimate Q3 at roughly $113.5 billion by applying the cited average 3.1% beat to the $110.16 billion high end of guidance, then carry the guided 12% sequential growth forward to Q4, about $127 billion, alongside reported Q1 and Q2 revenue. Supply constraints are a meaningful downside risk, but the cited estimate of more than $100 billion in unfulfilled demand is for “next year” and is not quantified as a reduction to FY2027 revenue. The implied outcome remains above $400 billion and below $450 billion, so it stays at rung 2.\nv16 2026-09-20 1596 1884 How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027? The reference is NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3. The most likely total is just above $25 billion: the specific FY2027 Vera outlook is about $20 billion, and Grace had already exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis during FY2027; carrying that recent Grace run-rate through the fiscal year puts the total slightly above the rung-1 boundary. Vera shipments and full production support the outlook, though the estimate is close to the boundary: Wolfe’s 2026 shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, and AMD’s performance claims are competitive risks without a quantified revision to NVIDIA’s FY2027 outlook. The approximately $20 billion server-CPU demand figure is a headwind, but it is not an explicit cap on NVIDIA’s specific Vera sales outlook.\nv17 2026-09-20 1586 1884 How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2028? The company’s approximately 70% FY2028 growth guidance is the primary reference and sits at rung 1; the more recent analyst estimates of 63.2%–65.5% also sit at rung 1, while the 44% LSEG estimate is a rung-3 counterweight. The implied outcome remains about 70%: NVIDIA says supply supports that growth, while demand is higher. The newer forecast of twice as many chips sold in calendar 2027 supports strong volume but does not translate directly into FY2028 revenue growth or displace the company’s supply-constrained outlook. The implied outcome stays above the 60% rung boundary.\nv21 2026-09-20 1599 1884 How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027? The market reference is roughly 87–90% FY2027 growth (Zacks estimates 87.4–88%, while the $411 billion estimate implies about 90% against FY2026 revenue), which is rung 1; the ladder's cited 42% estimate is not the current reference. Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance total $285.8 billion. Carrying forward the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion, FY2027 revenue near $407 billion, and growth of about 88% from FY2026's $215.94 billion. The CFO's approximately 70% growth outlook implies about $367 billion, also rung 1. Supplier constraints temper the faster run-rate case, but the reported results and Q3 guide still imply growth well above the 60% boundary.\nv35 2026-09-20 1608 1884 How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2028? The market reference is analyst consensus of about $678 billion for FY2028, which sits at rung 2. The current implied outcome remains around $671–$678 billion: NVIDIA's 70% outlook is about $673 billion, while the analyst forecast is $678 billion; applying Zacks' 63.2% growth estimate to the cited $411 billion FY2027 estimate gives about $671 billion. These figures remain below the $700 billion rung-1 boundary. Chip-volume and customer-demand evidence, together with favorable export-control and capex themes, support the upper end, but supply and infrastructure constraints, earlier Huawei launches, and memory tightness temper the upside; none supplies a quantified revision that takes FY2028 revenue across $700 billion.\nshift 2026-09-12 The reference is approximately $372 billion, which sits in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $414 billion: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; the $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide, applying the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $99.9 billion for Q3. Retaining the prior H2 analyst baseline of about $208 billion and applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside gives about $249.6 billion for H2, or roughly $414 billion for FY2027. This clears rung 2’s $400 billion flo\nshift 2026-09-13 The market reference is approximately $372 billion, which sits at rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $414 billion: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; the $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide implies about $100 billion of Data Center revenue at the latest 92.5% mix, and the carried H2 analyst baseline of about $208 billion, raised 20% by SemiAnalysis's H2 forecast, implies about $249.6 billion for H2. Q3 is included in H2, so it is not added again. The resulting FY2027 est\nshift 2026-09-14 The approximately $414 billion forecast is above rung 2’s $400 billion floor but below rung 1’s $450 billion floor. Strong growth, the Q3 guide, and demand evidence support the estimate, but the new customer commitments span multiple years and cannot be counted in full for FY2027. China revenue is excluded from NVIDIA’s forward outlook, and competing accelerator supply is a further offset; the evidence does not imply enough incremental FY2027 revenue to cross $450 billion.\nshift 2026-09-15 The reference is an approximately $414 billion FY2027 expectation, which sits in rung 2. The latest reported quarter and near-term outlook imply a lower run-rate: Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; applying the recent 92.5% Data Center mix to NVIDIA's $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide gives about $100 billion for Q3. Carrying that growth into Q4, with management's stated Q4 reacceleration, implies roughly $115 billion for Q4 and about $379 billion for FY2027. The demand evidence and Ru\n414 items []\ncounts [('v9', 8), ('v8', 8), ('v5', 5), ('v10', 6)]\nrank v43 Will the required U.S. and EU regulatory reviews of NVIDIA's Hugging Face acquisition be cleared by 2027-06-30?\nrank v42 Will the AI Energy Management Alliance's first power-flexible AI data center in Manassas secure faster grid interconnection terms or preferential tariffs by 2026-12-31?\nrank v40 Will NVIDIA invest $10 billion to anchor Anthropic's IPO by 2026-09-20?\nrank v39 Will the U.S. Justice Department conclude its antitrust review of NVIDIA’s Groq transaction by 2027-01-31?\nrank v38 Will NVIDIA complete an investment of roughly $2.5 billion to $3 billion in Thinking Machines Lab by 2027-09-04?\nold 3 views 49 deps 0 judgements 1707 citing others 0\nv3 2026-09-20 1672 1884 What will NVIDIA report as total revenue for FY2027? The market reference is about $411 billion (e4360, e5406), which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The evidence-based run-rate is about $419 billion before the additional second-half data-center-compute uplift: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion; Q3 is about $113.6 billion using the $108 billion midpoint guidance and the cited 3.1% average beat over the high end; carrying the guided 12% sequential growth pace forward gives about $127 billion for Q4. Applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift only to second-half data-center compute adds about $27–28 billion, implying roughly $446 billion and crossing the rung-1 boundary. The analyst view of 90% Q3 and 65% Q4 year-over-year growth is a counter-signal, but it does not outweigh the guided-growth run-rate and the specific second-half compute tracking. The 70% full-year growth statement would imply about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue, but that would require a sharp Q4 decline after the reported first half and Q3 guidance, so it is inconsistent with the nearer-term run-rate.\ncurrent band {'position': 1, 'label': 'Strongly bullish', 'condition': 'Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion (110% of the $392 billion analyst estimate).', 'lo': 431200000000.0, 'hi': None}\nv35 2026-09-20 1674 1884 What will NVIDIA report as data-center revenue for FY2027? The only supplied quantified market reference is Morningstar's $361 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits exactly at rung 2's floor. The implied outcome is about $374–375 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion in the first half; the Q3 outlook of about $108 billion in total revenue, applied to the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $100 billion of Data Center revenue; and roughly $110 billion in Q4 is consistent with management's expected reacceleration and the supplied forecast of more than $100 billion. That is above rung 2's $361 billion floor but below rung 1's $400 billion threshold; the new demand and supply evidence does not quantify enough additional FY2027 revenue to bridge the gap.\ncurrent band {'position': 2, 'label': 'Bullish', 'condition': 'FY2027 data-center revenue is at least $361 billion but less than $400 billion.', 'lo': 361000000000.0, 'hi': 400000000000.0}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3, re, json\nfrom pathlib import Path\ndef op(p):\n c=sqlite3.connect(Path(p).resolve().as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True); c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row; return c\nb='\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17'\\\\''\nfor i, vids in [(1,('\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v8'\\\\'')),(2,('\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v16'\\\\'','\\\\''v21'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\'')),(3,('\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\''))]:\n d=f'\\\\''{b}/run/old-{i}/prod/'\\\\''\n with op(d+'\\\\''views.sqlite'\\\\'') as v, op(d+'\\\\''archive.sqlite'\\\\'') as a:\n  for k in vids:\n   j=v.execute('\\\\''select * from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id desc limit 1'\\\\'',(k,)).fetchone()\n   r=a.execute('\\\\''select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],)).fetchone()\n   if not r: print('\\\\''no request'\\\\'',i,k,j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\'']); continue\n   req=json.loads(r[0]); print(i,k,'\\\\''last'\\\\'',j['\\\\''publication_day'\\\\''],j['\\\\''judgement_id'\\\\''],'\\\\''ref'\\\\'',j['\\\\''reference'\\\\''],'\\\\''implied'\\\\'',j['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''],'\\\\''len'\\\\'',len(j['\\\\''reasons'\\\\'']),'\\\\''r'\\\\'',j['\\\\''rung'\\\\''])\n   if k in (('\\\\''v4'\\\\'',) if i==1 else ('\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\'') if i==2 else ('\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\'')):\n    print('\\\\''request-keys'\\\\'',list(req) if isinstance(req,dict) else type(req))\n    print('\\\\''reasons'\\\\'',j['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''])\n    print('\\\\''dedicated dependency block'\\\\'', '\\\\''Current conclusions of the theses it depends on'\\\\'' in r[0], '\\\\''theme block'\\\\'', '\\\\''[Themes in force'\\\\'' in r[0])\n  if i==2:\n   for j in v.execute(\\\"select publication_day, reference,implied_outcome from judgements where view_id='\\\\''v9'\\\\'' and publication_day between '\\\\''2026-09-12'\\\\'' and '\\\\''2026-09-15'\\\\'' order by judgement_id\\\"): print(tuple(j))\nwith op(b+'\\\\''/items-comparison.sqlite'\\\\'') as c:\n for k, p in [('\\\\''gnews:f94df9a4bc981f0c32ba5797'\\\\'',r'\\\\''673 billion'\\\\''),('\\\\''gnews:3a284e233d8b31c62dc66b33'\\\\'',r'\\\\''70 percent'\\\\''),('\\\\''truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26'\\\\'',r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$20 billion|70%'\"'\\\\'')]:\n  t=c.execute('\\\\''select text from items where input_id=?'\\\\'',(k,)).fetchone()[0]\n  for m in list(re.finditer(p,t,re.I))[:3]: print(k,re.sub(r'\\\\''\\\\s+'\\\\'','\\\\'' '\\\\'',t[max(0,m.start()-120):m.end()+145]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3, re, json\nfrom pathlib import Path\ndef op(p):\n c=sqlite3.connect(Path(p).resolve().as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True); c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row; return c\nb='\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17'\\\\''\nfor i, vids in [(1,('\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v8'\\\\'')),(2,('\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v16'\\\\'','\\\\''v21'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\'')),(3,('\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\''))]:\n d=f'\\\\''{b}/run/old-{i}/prod/'\\\\''\n with op(d+'\\\\''views.sqlite'\\\\'') as v, op(d+'\\\\''archive.sqlite'\\\\'') as a:\n  for k in vids:\n   j=v.execute('\\\\''select * from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id desc limit 1'\\\\'',(k,)).fetchone()\n   r=a.execute('\\\\''select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],)).fetchone()\n   if not r: print('\\\\''no request'\\\\'',i,k,j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\'']); continue\n   req=json.loads(r[0]); print(i,k,'\\\\''last'\\\\'',j['\\\\''publication_day'\\\\''],j['\\\\''judgement_id'\\\\''],'\\\\''ref'\\\\'',j['\\\\''reference'\\\\''],'\\\\''implied'\\\\'',j['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''],'\\\\''len'\\\\'',len(j['\\\\''reasons'\\\\'']),'\\\\''r'\\\\'',j['\\\\''rung'\\\\''])\n   if k in (('\\\\''v4'\\\\'',) if i==1 else ('\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\'') if i==2 else ('\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\'')):\n    print('\\\\''request-keys'\\\\'',list(req) if isinstance(req,dict) else type(req))\n    print('\\\\''reasons'\\\\'',j['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''])\n    print('\\\\''dedicated dependency block'\\\\'', '\\\\''Current conclusions of the theses it depends on'\\\\'' in r[0], '\\\\''theme block'\\\\'', '\\\\''[Themes in force'\\\\'' in r[0])\n  if i==2:\n   for j in v.execute(\\\"select publication_day, reference,implied_outcome from judgements where view_id='\\\\''v9'\\\\'' and publication_day between '\\\\''2026-09-12'\\\\'' and '\\\\''2026-09-15'\\\\'' order by judgement_id\\\"): print(tuple(j))\nwith op(b+'\\\\''/items-comparison.sqlite'\\\\'') as c:\n for k, p in [('\\\\''gnews:f94df9a4bc981f0c32ba5797'\\\\'',r'\\\\''673 billion'\\\\''),('\\\\''gnews:3a284e233d8b31c62dc66b33'\\\\'',r'\\\\''70 percent'\\\\''),('\\\\''truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26'\\\\'',r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$20 billion|70%'\"'\\\\'')]:\n  t=c.execute('\\\\''select text from items where input_id=?'\\\\'',(k,)).fetchone()[0]\n  for m in list(re.finditer(p,t,re.I))[:3]: print(k,re.sub(r'\\\\''\\\\s+'\\\\'','\\\\'' '\\\\'',t[max(0,m.start()-120):m.end()+145]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"1 v4 last 2026-09-20 1575 ref Wall Street consensus is about $411 billion for FY2027, in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $367 billion, in rung 3. implied About $404 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.62 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, an estimated Q3 of about $113.6 billion (the cited 3.1% historical beat applied to the $110.16 billion high end of guidance), and Q4 of about $112.2 billion (65% growth on Q4 FY2026's $68 billion actual). len 533 r 2\nrequest-keys ['max_completion_tokens', 'messages', 'model', 'reasoning_effort', 'response_format']\nreasons The market reference is Wall Street consensus of about $411 billion, which is rung 2. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion (rung 3), but the current-quarter guidance and the specific Q4 growth estimate imply a higher outcome: about $404 billion. That is above rung 3's $392 billion boundary but below rung 1's $431.2 billion threshold. Supply constraints and competition temper the upside; the evidence does not imply enough additional revenue to cross rung 1.\ndedicated dependency block False theme block True\n1 v8 last 2026-09-20 1580 ref Morningstar estimates FY2027 NVIDIA Data Center revenue at $361 billion; that is rung 2 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). implied About $390 billion: $164 billion reported in the first half, about $100 billion in Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guidance midpoint and 92.5% recent Data Center mix, plus an estimated $126 billion in Q4 as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Vera Rubin supply builds, discounted for continuing constraints. len 626 r 2\n2 v9 last 2026-09-20 1592 ref The market reference supplied by the previous conclusion is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2 ($400 billion to below $450 billion). implied Approximately $410 billion: $164 billion reported in the first half, about $100 billion implied for Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and roughly $146 billion estimated for Q4. The Q4 estimate reflects management's expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin ramp, with a discounted contribution from SemiAnalysis's forecast that second-half Data Center revenue would exceed analyst estimates by 20%. len 1044 r 2\nrequest-keys ['max_completion_tokens', 'messages', 'model', 'reasoning_effort', 'response_format']\nreasons The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is now around $410 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion in the first half, Q3 total-revenue guidance and the recent Data Center mix imply roughly $100 billion for Q3, and a roughly $146 billion Q4 is supported by the expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin ramp. SemiAnalysis's forecast of second-half Data Center revenue 20% above analyst estimates is a meaningful upside input: applying it to the roughly $250 billion of second-half revenue implied by the $414 billion reference would point to about $464 billion for the year. I discount that forecast because it does not state its underlying estimate and its implied Q4 is much higher than the company has quantified; the resulting central estimate remains near $410 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. Supply and competition risks, and NVIDIA's exclusion of China compute revenue from its outlook, temper the upside.\ndedicated dependency block False theme block True\n2 v14 last 2026-09-20 1585 ref Market expectation is about $411 billion for FY2027, rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion). Zacks' 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of approximately $215.9 billion implies about $405 billion, also rung 2. implied About $419 billion: Q1 $81.6 billion plus Q2 $96.2 billion, estimated Q3 of about $113.5 billion (the $110.16 billion guidance high end plus the reported average 3.1% beat), and Q4 of about $127 billion by carrying forward the guided 12% sequential growth. len 755 r 2\nrequest-keys ['max_completion_tokens', 'messages', 'model', 'reasoning_effort', 'response_format']\nreasons The reference is about $411 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2; the cited Zacks growth estimate also implies about $405 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate implies about $419 billion: estimate Q3 at roughly $113.5 billion by applying the cited average 3.1% beat to the $110.16 billion high end of guidance, then carry the guided 12% sequential growth forward to Q4, about $127 billion, alongside reported Q1 and Q2 revenue. Supply constraints are a meaningful downside risk, but the cited estimate of more than $100 billion in unfulfilled demand is for “next year” and is not quantified as a reduction to FY2027 revenue. The implied outcome remains above $400 billion and below $450 billion, so it stays at rung 2.\ndedicated dependency block False theme block True\n2 v16 last 2026-09-20 1596 ref NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion falls at rung 3. implied Approximately $25 billion or slightly more for FY2027: about $20 billion from Vera standalone CPU sales plus a roughly $5 billion Grace contribution, using Grace’s reported greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue as the recent run-rate proxy. len 834 r 1\n2 v21 last 2026-09-20 1599 ref Wall Street's current FY2027 growth expectations are about 87–90% (Zacks: 87.4–88%; $411 billion revenue implies about 90% versus FY2026), placing the reference at rung 1. The ladder's cited 42% estimate is not the current market reference. implied Q1 and Q2 revenue plus the Q3 guidance midpoint total $285.8 billion; extending the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion, or approximately $407 billion for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies about 88% growth; the CFO's approximately 70% full-year outlook is a lower check, implying about $367 billion, but also falls in rung 1. len 723 r 1\n2 v35 last 2026-09-20 1608 ref Analyst consensus of about $678 billion for FY2028 (e4348), which is rung 2. Zacks' 63.2% FY2028 growth estimate (e4703), applied to the cited $411 billion FY2027 estimate, implies about $671 billion and is also in rung 2. implied About $671–$678 billion: NVIDIA's 70% FY2028 growth outlook implies about $673 billion, analyst estimates put FY2028 at $678 billion, and applying Zacks' 63.2% growth estimate to the cited $411 billion FY2027 base gives about $671 billion. len 727 r 2\n('2026-09-12', 'Market consensus is approximately $372 billion, the reference supplied in the previous conclusion; that is rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).', 'Approximately $414 billion: Q1 plus Q2 Data Center revenue is $164.25 billion; Q3 is estimated at about $99.9 billion from the $108 billion company revenue guide and the latest 92.5% Data Center mix. The prior H2 baseline of about $208 billion, raised 20% in line with SemiAnalysis’s H2 estimate, implies about $249.6 billion for H2 and roughly $414 billion for the year.')\n('2026-09-13', 'Approximately $372 billion, the market reference carried from the prior conclusion; this sits at rung 3.', 'About $414 billion: $164.25 billion from Q1 and Q2 plus approximately $249.6 billion for H2, using the carried $208 billion H2 analyst baseline increased 20% by SemiAnalysis. The Q3 guide and current Data Center mix imply about $100 billion for Q3, which is part of H2, not an additional amount.')\n('2026-09-14', 'The market reference carried from the previous judgement is approximately $372 billion, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).', 'Approximately $414 billion: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total about $164.25 billion; the $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide implies about $100 billion of Data Center revenue at the latest reported mix. Carrying forward the prior H2 baseline of about $208 billion and applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% above-estimates forecast gives about $249.6 billion for H2, including Q3, and about $414 billion for FY2027.')\n('2026-09-15', 'Approximately $414 billion is the reference expectation, in rung 2 ($400 billion to below $450 billion).', \"About $379 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2, about $100 billion in Q3 (the $108 billion total-revenue guide multiplied by the recent 92.5% Data Center mix), and roughly $115 billion in Q4, allowing for management's stated reacceleration. The total falls in rung 3.\")\n3 v3 last 2026-09-20 1672 ref Wall Street analysts expect about $411 billion for FY2027 (e4360, e5406), placing the reference in rung 2. implied About $446 billion: $177.837 billion in first-half revenue, approximately $113.6 billion for Q3 using the guidance midpoint and the cited average beat, and approximately $127 billion for Q4 by carrying forward 12% sequential growth, then adding about $27–28 billion for the estimated 20% second-half data-center-compute uplift. len 1054 r 1\nrequest-keys ['max_completion_tokens', 'messages', 'model', 'reasoning_effort', 'response_format']\nreasons The market reference is about $411 billion (e4360, e5406), which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The evidence-based run-rate is about $419 billion before the additional second-half data-center-compute uplift: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion; Q3 is about $113.6 billion using the $108 billion midpoint guidance and the cited 3.1% average beat over the high end; carrying the guided 12% sequential growth pace forward gives about $127 billion for Q4. Applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift only to second-half data-center compute adds about $27–28 billion, implying roughly $446 billion and crossing the rung-1 boundary. The analyst view of 90% Q3 and 65% Q4 year-over-year growth is a counter-signal, but it does not outweigh the guided-growth run-rate and the specific second-half compute tracking. The 70% full-year growth statement would imply about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue, but that would require a sharp Q4 decline after the reported first half and Q3 guidance, so it is inconsistent with the nearer-term run-rate.\ndedicated dependency block False theme block True\n3 v35 last 2026-09-20 1674 ref The only supplied quantified market reference is Morningstar's $361 billion FY2027 estimate (e2295), at the floor of rung 2 ($361 billion to less than $400 billion). implied About $374–375 billion: $164 billion reported in the first half, approximately $100 billion implied for Q3 by the $108 billion total-revenue outlook and the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, plus roughly $110 billion for Q4 given expected reacceleration and the forecast of more than $100 billion. len 706 r 2\nrequest-keys ['max_completion_tokens', 'messages', 'model', 'reasoning_effort', 'response_format']\nreasons The only supplied quantified market reference is Morningstar's $361 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits exactly at rung 2's floor. The implied outcome is about $374–375 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion in the first half; the Q3 outlook of about $108 billion in total revenue, applied to the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $100 billion of Data Center revenue; and roughly $110 billion in Q4 is consistent with management's expected reacceleration and the supplied forecast of more than $100 billion. That is above rung 2's $361 billion floor but below rung 1's $400 billion threshold; the new demand and supply evidence does not quantify enough additional FY2027 revenue to bridge the gap.\ndedicated dependency block False theme block True\ngnews:f94df9a4bc981f0c32ba5797 mes shortly after the company revealed that it expected 70% growth in the fiscal year ending in January 2028, or about $673 billion, which caused analyst expectations to shoot up. \"I expect Nvidia to sell twice as many chips as this next year as we do this year,\" Huang told t\ngnews:3a284e233d8b31c62dc66b33 ut financial assistance. “I don’t think that the need or the desire is going to go anywhere,” he said. Nvidia Forecasts 70 Percent Of Supply-Constrained Growth In 2027 During Nvidia’s second-quarter earnings call last month, the company surprised Wall Street by disclosing it\ngnews:3a284e233d8b31c62dc66b33  call last month, the company surprised Wall Street by disclosing its revenue growth forecast for next year: a whopping 70 percent. This raises the prospect that Nvidia—which financial analysts on average estimate will make $411 billion in revenue for its current fiscal year\ngnews:3a284e233d8b31c62dc66b33 ers’ forecasts point to our growth doubling next year. However, as I mentioned earlier, we expect to grow approximately 70 percent as we are supply constrained.” This suggests that Nvidia could leave demand unfulfilled for more than $100 billion in AI infrastructure gear, wh\ntruenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 ning hyperscalers, AI labs, AI natives, enterprises and sovereign customers. We expect to grow revenue by approximately 70% in fiscal 2028. This is a supply-constrained outlook. Q2 data center revenue increased 18% quarter-over-quarter to $89 billion with strong contr\ntruenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 ers forecasts point to our growth, doubling next year. However, as I mentioned earlier, we expect to grow approximately 70% as we are supply constrained. NVIDIA compute is fully utilized across every cloud we serve, the economic value it generates for hyperscale, NeoC\ntruenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 our lead partners, including OCI, SpaceX AI and starting this quarter, AWS. We continue to see demand for approximately $20 billion in total server CPUs. And based on our customer demand and improving supply outlook, our preliminary expectation is for CPU revenue to more than\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"agent_message","text":"The finished run confirms the first contradiction, and it’s larger than the brief says. The stored company-wide Q4 forecast is $112.2B, while Data Center alone is $126B. I’ll check what the proposed test would actually show the receiving question."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '570,591p' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/library-pack.md; rg -n 'The thesis tree:|narrower.*root|root.*narrower' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | head -8\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '570,591p' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/library-pack.md; rg -n 'The thesis tree:|narrower.*root|root.*narrower' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | head -8\"","aggregated_output":"  - e668 [party_statement; 2026-07-21; push toward_first] NVIDIA said OpenAI planned to deploy Vera CPUs in large quantities starting in calendar Q3 2026. | source: Nvidia details its next-generation Vera CPU for AI, setting up challenge to AMD and Intel (cnbc.com) input gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c\n  - e723 [fact; 2026-07-21; push toward_first] NVIDIA said it delivered Vera chips to OpenAI, Anthropic, and SpaceX in June 2026. | source: Nvidia is releasing full specs for its AI server CPU, taking aim at AMD and Intel (qz.com) input gnews:9ec05dc5e996b554b6f7a5f2\n  - e724 [fact; 2026-07-21; push toward_first] Hewlett Packard Enterprise unveiled the Vera-powered ProLiant Compute DL394 Gen12 server, with availability expected in fall 2026. | source: Nvidia is releasing full specs for its AI server CPU, taking aim at AMD and Intel (qz.com) input gnews:9ec05dc5e996b554b6f7a5f2\n  - e794 [third_party_view; 2026-07-22; push toward_last] AMD estimates EPYC 9965 delivers approximately 2.4 times the rack-level throughput of NVIDIA Vera in a modeled 100kW deployment, and projects EPYC 6 at roughly 3.3 times Vera's total throughput. | source: BofA sees server CPU TAM hitting $170bn by 2030 as NVIDIA takes on AMD (au.finance.yahoo.com) input gnews:55b3b854d30ecb73679c2088\n  - e896 [party_statement; 2026-07-24; push none] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera opens a $200 billion market for NVIDIA and that the company has visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, FY2027. | source: NVIDIA Says Its CPU Revenue Sits Outside the $1 Trillion Forecast. Here’s Where the Stock Could Go (tikr.com) input gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b\n  - e897 [party_statement; 2026-07-24; push toward_first] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the nearly $20 billion CPU revenue visibility for FY2027 is for standalone CPU sales. | source: NVIDIA Says Its CPU Revenue Sits Outside the $1 Trillion Forecast. Here’s Where the Stock Could Go (tikr.com) input gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b\n  - e2311 [party_statement; 2026-08-24; push toward_first] NVIDIA expects stand-alone Vera server CPU revenue of $20 billion in FY2027. | source: Where Will Nvidia Stock Be in 5 Years? (fool.com) input gnews:6c6c4a4aa3b22bf433133e4d\n  - e2542 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] NVIDIA Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis as of the Q2 FY2027 call. | source: NVDA Q2 FY2027 Earnings Call (NVIDIA Corporation) input truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26\n  - e2543 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] NVIDIA said Vera CPU shipments were underway to lead partners including OCI and SpaceX AI, with AWS shipments starting in Q2 FY2027, and expected deployment by every major hyperscaler, NeoCloud, AI lab, and system OEM. | source: NVDA Q2 FY2027 Earnings Call (NVIDIA Corporation) input truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26\n  - e2544 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] NVIDIA estimated total server CPU demand at approximately $20 billion and preliminarily expected its CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028. | source: NVDA Q2 FY2027 Earnings Call (NVIDIA Corporation) input truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26\n  - e2636 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] Jensen Huang said Amazon Web Services was going to buy millions of CPUs; no purchase period or exact quantity was specified. | source: CNBC | VERSANT Press Room (cnbc.com) input extra:b23f73a08a535993119430f5\n  - e2735 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] NVIDIA said Vera CPU was in full production and expected total CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028, citing customer demand and an improving supply outlook. | source: NVIDIA Q2 Earnings Call Highlights (marketbeat.com) input gnews:7dfab0010a8ac15786f05387\n  - e3007 [fact; 2026-08-27; push toward_first] Amazon Web Services may buy millions of NVIDIA Vera CPUs; the purchase is conditional and no date was specified. | source: Jim Cramer says Nvidia and Salesforce earnings upended two bear narratives (cnbc.com) input gnews:fa03217ed7ae3070a82acd79\n  - e3087 [fact; 2026-08-28; push toward_first] AWS agreed to buy 2 million NVIDIA GPUs and adopt NVIDIA's Vera CPU, with some GPUs integrated with Rubin. | source: NVIDIA (NVDA) Tops Q2 Estimates and Guides Above Forecasts, But Shares Slip (insidermonkey.com) input gnews:d341dec96cd1045ab0e0a92f\n  - e4398 [party_statement; 2026-09-10; push toward_first] NVIDIA was already shipping Vera CPUs to key customers and expected $20 billion in Vera CPU sales in FY2027. | source: An Nvidia Move Just Put AMD and Intel on the Defensive (fool.com) input gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a\n  - e4797 [party_statement; 2026-09-16; push toward_last] NVIDIA estimates the total annual server CPU market at approximately $20 billion. | source: Why It’s Time to Load Up on Nvidia Stock (barchart.com) input gnews:5c5a96c670714dee86769d87\n  - e5047 [party_statement; 2026-09-19; push toward_last] AMD reported that EPYC Venice delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026 testing. | source: AMD Targets NVIDIA Vera In Latest EPYC Venice “Agentic AI” Benchmarks, Zen 6 Leads With 20% Higher Per-Core & Over 2x Gains In Platform-Level Performance (wccftech.com) input gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674\n  - e5048 [party_statement; 2026-09-19; push toward_last] AMD reported EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2x the per-core performance and 2.24x the platform-level performance of a Vera-based platform in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing. | source: AMD Targets NVIDIA Vera In Latest EPYC Venice “Agentic AI” Benchmarks, Zen 6 Leads With 20% Higher Per-Core & Over 2x Gains In Platform-Level Performance (wccftech.com) input gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674\n  - e5049 [party_statement; 2026-09-19; push toward_last] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system delivered 3.4x the throughput of a Vera-based platform in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack. | source: AMD Targets NVIDIA Vera In Latest EPYC Venice “Agentic AI” Benchmarks, Zen 6 Leads With 20% Higher Per-Core & Over 2x Gains In Platform-Level Performance (wccftech.com) input gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674\n  - e5050 [party_statement; 2026-09-19; push toward_last] AMD reported 37% higher overall SPEC 2026 performance for 192-core 5th Gen EPYC CPUs and 2.37x performance for 256-core 6th Gen EPYC Venice versus NVIDIA Vera. | source: AMD Targets NVIDIA Vera In Latest EPYC Venice “Agentic AI” Benchmarks, Zen 6 Leads With 20% Higher Per-Core & Over 2x Gains In Platform-Level Performance (wccftech.com) input gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674\n  - e5051 [party_statement; 2026-09-19; push toward_last] AMD reported EPYC 9006 CPUs achieved a 12% per-core performance gain in Agentic AI benchmarks, averaging 20% in the a4 subtests. | source: AMD Targets NVIDIA Vera In Latest EPYC Venice “Agentic AI” Benchmarks, Zen 6 Leads With 20% Higher Per-Core & Over 2x Gains In Platform-Level Performance (wccftech.com) input gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674\n  - e5052 [party_statement; 2026-09-19; push toward_last] AMD’s Agentic AI benchmark results showed EPYC Venice up to 2.01x faster per core in stockfish_r, with a lead above 30% in many tests against NVIDIA Vera. | source: AMD Targets NVIDIA Vera In Latest EPYC Venice “Agentic AI” Benchmarks, Zen 6 Leads With 20% Higher Per-Core & Over 2x Gains In Platform-Level Performance (wccftech.com) input gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674\n2638:## D208 · 2026-10-28 · The thesis tree: root quantities, a seeded driver layer per company, shared macro themes with declared channels (design, not built)\n3082:- Measured (six draws finished 19:40; `e2e-funnel-r6-v72.txt`, `judgement-gold/score-r6-*`, `matter-states-r6-v72.txt`, `probe-v72-sink.txt`; three-draw means, v91 → v92). Regressions first: core matters reaching judgement 76.3 → 73.7 of 86 (predicted ≥ +2, the null is ±2), core dropped for no home 4.0 → 5.7, article-opened questions 11.7 → 3.0 (candidates 65 → 6.7: the list shown suppresses proposals even with the candidate rule unchanged; must_open 28/28 and may_open 40/42 in both arms because the seeds cover them, the Ohio guarantee being the one visible loss), supporting not extracted 25.3 → 46.0. Held: supporting reaching 94.0 → 90.0 (at the −4 line), noise reaching 89.2 → 56.9 (−36%), judgement agreement 17/19, 17/18, 17/19 → 15/18, 16/17, 17/18 against lookup 8–9/14–15, cost $1.15 → $0.88 a draw with model time −36% and wall clock 95 → 46 minutes for three draws, zero re-sent requests. Two mechanisms, both in sentences and neither in the architecture: (1) the sink is back: 60–62% of links on the three company-wide revenue questions against v91's 34–40%, 190–208 statements a draw under both total and Data Center revenue against 5–6; the model's own account in three of three cases cites the figure-line takes clause D259 added (\"what makes that figure more or less likely: the demand, supply, competition and pricing behind it\") and concedes the links field's \"narrowest only\" should have excluded the broader question or the link altogether; (2) role-v66's end-of-request check removes 21 supporting matters a draw that v53 keeps (20 matters extracted in v91 in at least two draws and in v92 in at most one: third parties' actions and views, named hires and partners, product milestones, standard §5.1 kinds 2–3), a loss the extraction bench's \"supporting in at least two draws\" count had hidden. Verdict: as built, v72 is neither better nor worse than v91 on the desk's counts and is cleaner, cheaper and faster; it does not become the default and no further arm is sent before the two mechanisms are fixed at their owners with $0 proofs: the root/driver pair collapsed to the narrower question by code (identity, D207's rule), the figure-line clause reworded and proved on re-sent stored v92 requests, and the base text (v53 against v66 under the architecture) put to Alex.\n3088:- The first fix, code, $0 (the sink of D264): `extraction.narrowest_links`, applied in `file_reading` to every filing from v20 on: a statement linked to a question and to a question that question depends on (the ledger's `dependencies`, the seeds' root-and-driver pairs, transitively) keeps the narrower one only, which is D207's \"narrowest question only\" made an identity rule where the ledger knows the hierarchy. Replayed over the three v92 ledgers: links 967/921/907 → removed 302/310/285 (31–34%), no statement left without a link (the rule keeps the narrower); the three revenue questions' share and the per-question piles after the rule are printed in `design-v72.md` §9. Test `test_a_statement_filed_under_a_driver_and_its_root_keeps_the_driver_only`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
