{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor i,vs in ((1,('\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v8'\\\\'')),(2,('\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\'')),(3,('\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\''))):\n p=Path(f'\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{i}/prod/views.sqlite'\\\\'').resolve()\n with sqlite3.connect(p.as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True) as c:\n  for v in vs:\n   print(i,v,c.execute('\\\\''select substr(reasons,1,600) from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id desc limit 1'\\\\'',(v,)).fetchone()[0])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor i,vs in ((1,('\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v8'\\\\'')),(2,('\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\'')),(3,('\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\''))):\n p=Path(f'\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{i}/prod/views.sqlite'\\\\'').resolve()\n with sqlite3.connect(p.as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True) as c:\n  for v in vs:\n   print(i,v,c.execute('\\\\''select substr(reasons,1,600) from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id desc limit 1'\\\\'',(v,)).fetchone()[0])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"1 v4 The market reference is Wall Street consensus of about $411 billion, which is rung 2. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion (rung 3), but the current-quarter guidance and the specific Q4 growth estimate imply a higher outcome: about $404 billion. That is above rung 3's $392 billion boundary but below rung 1's $431.2 billion threshold. Supply constraints and competition temper the upside; the evidence does not imply enough additional revenue to cross rung 1.\n1 v8 The reference is Morningstar’s $361 billion FY2027 Data Center estimate, which is in rung 2. The implied outcome is higher, at about $390 billion, but remains below rung 1’s $400 billion boundary: the reported first half is $164 billion, Q3 is about $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue midpoint and the recent 92.5% Data Center mix, and I estimate Q4 at about $126 billion, allowing for the stated Q4 reacceleration and Vera Rubin supply build while discounting for supply and infrastructure constraints. Thus the evidence supports a substantial premium to the reference but does not im\n2 v14 The reference is about $411 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2; the cited Zacks growth estimate also implies about $405 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate implies about $419 billion: estimate Q3 at roughly $113.5 billion by applying the cited average 3.1% beat to the $110.16 billion high end of guidance, then carry the guided 12% sequential growth forward to Q4, about $127 billion, alongside reported Q1 and Q2 revenue. Supply constraints are a meaningful downside risk, but the cited estimate of more than $100 billion in unfulfilled demand is for “next \n2 v9 The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is now around $410 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion in the first half, Q3 total-revenue guidance and the recent Data Center mix imply roughly $100 billion for Q3, and a roughly $146 billion Q4 is supported by the expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin ramp. SemiAnalysis's forecast of second-half Data Center revenue 20% above analyst estimates is a meaningful upside input: applying it to the roughly $250 billion of second-half revenue implied by the $414 billion reference would\n3 v3 The market reference is about $411 billion (e4360, e5406), which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The evidence-based run-rate is about $419 billion before the additional second-half data-center-compute uplift: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion; Q3 is about $113.6 billion using the $108 billion midpoint guidance and the cited 3.1% average beat over the high end; carrying the guided 12% sequential growth pace forward gives about $127 billion for Q4. Applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift only to second-half data-center compute adds about $27–28 billion, implying roughly $\n3 v35 The only supplied quantified market reference is Morningstar's $361 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits exactly at rung 2's floor. The implied outcome is about $374–375 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion in the first half; the Q3 outlook of about $108 billion in total revenue, applied to the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $100 billion of Data Center revenue; and roughly $110 billion in Q4 is consistent with management's expected reacceleration and the supplied forecast of more than $100 billion. That is above rung 2's $361 billion floor but below rung 1's $400 billion threshold\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"agent_message","text":"**Fix the conflicting forecasts first. Defer the daily wrapper.** A daily refresh would still leave an analyst choosing between $146B for Data Center and $127B for the whole company.\n\n**(a) What holds up**\n\n1. **v9’s $414B came from its own forecast.** It was the card’s estimate September 12–14 and became its “reference” September 15. I found no v9 evidence item carrying that figure.\n2. **Old-2 has two more inconsistencies and a period error.** v16 assumes $20B from standalone Vera alone while v8 estimates $18B for all standalone CPUs. v35’s $673B at 70% growth uses a ~$396B base. Applying that growth to v14’s $419B gives ~$712B, above v35’s band. v21 uses the 70% figure as a FY2027 cross-check. The earnings call explicitly says FY2028.\n3. **Old-1 is worse than stated.** The saved answer gives $112.2B for Q4 total, versus $126B for Data Center. Old-3’s $446B total and $374–375B segment imply about 84% Data Center mix. That divergence needs explaining, especially since the total adds $27–28B for Data Center compute. A 92.5% mix is an assumption, so this is weaker evidence than a segment exceeding the whole.\n4. **The missing links are real.** Across all three runs, zero of 139 cards had dependencies and zero of 4,932 judgements cited one. The requests still saw shared themes. The records establish that the revenue cards lacked each other’s forecasts. Whether sharing them fixes the answers remains open.\n\nThe default page order does put Hugging Face clearance and Manassas first. Revenue is also reachable by scrolling. [Library ordering](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/ui/src/model/library.js:32)\n\n**(b) What matters first.** Reconcile the core forecasts, then fill the missing big bets, then address unexplained forecast moves and evidence clutter. Eight moves alone tell me little. Recycling an old forecast as market evidence tells me why one move deserves scrutiny.\n\n**(c) First proof.** Start with a free check of the exact text available before each call. D368 sends the narrower driver’s conclusion to the broader question. Claude’s proposed test sends it the other way, and the existing dependency text would omit old-1’s $112.2B figure. If the right-direction text carries the decisive number, the cents test is worthwhile as a diagnostic: compare with fresh unchanged controls and require source-backed quarterly totals that reconcile. Forcing a 92.5% mix or feeding back a later answer could manufacture agreement. [D368](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3968) · [Dependency text](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:137)\n\n**CONSENSUS**"}}
